<p>Oh, just two simple tags. That's refreshingly easy.</p>
<p>Edit that I was going to commit somewhere in the first section:</p>
<p>Bear in mind the Apollos <em>looked</em> like they took off slowly, but that's only because they were <em>very long</em>, like 100m+. By the time the rocket outlets passed the top of the launch tower, they were already doing considerably more than 100mph. 1G acceleration is already good for 0-60mph in about 3 seconds, but a typical manned launch pulls more like 3G. Scaled down to give the same initially slow appearance, the bottle rocket may take a few seconds to reach human walking pace, but like a freight train it'll keep on accelerating, and a walking human can cover 15 metres quite quickly. It's about the length of a small house plus a token front and rear garden, and typical walking speed is several drinks bottle lengths per second.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<p>Though, please don't take any of this as a problem with the idea itself, or the writeup in general. It's quite delightful otherwise, just the scaling needs attention (and even that seems correct for the size of the bugs at least, as well as the size of a facility that could be easily obscured within a floridian forest).</p>

